# looper-recap: the nightly recap (looper#57; README: Nightly recap). Jody,
# 2026-11-02: "Each night we should run a daily recap and identify if there is
# anything that can be done to improve performance, eliminate bottle necks, etc.
# ... If something is just transient then we just note it and move on. I am
# looking for actionalbe advice". The roadmap's nightly scan (the suggestion
# pass, looper#50) runs it: the data is gathered and classified here, with no
# model; the model only writes the advice, as suggestions, from the numbers.
#
#   looper-recap collect [--until TIME] [--out FILE]
#       the 24 hours to TIME (default: now, to the minute), as one JSON file
#       (stdout without --out): the same inputs give the same file. For every
#       finished job of LOOPER_RECAP_PROJECTS (default LOOPER_SUGGEST_PROJECTS,
#       else tacplus-dev/looper tacplus-dev/build, plus tacplus-dev/cluster):
#       its queue (created to started), prepare (started to "Running on", the
#       method of runner-image measure) and run times, its status and a failed
#       one's class (runner_system_failure, image_pull, timeout, gate_failure,
#       script_failure, or other:<GitLab's reason>), and whether a retry of it
#       succeeded. From the looper jobs' logs, per repository: slot use (each
#       plan's children against its capacity), the held reasons with their
#       counts and hours, the lands' times by gate set and the deferred lands,
#       and how long each scan held the repository; and the hours each ready
#       issue waited, and why: no free slot, a conflict (and what overlaps), the
#       job's time left (its limit would outlast the job), queued behind a land
#       (the lander's "queued since"), a scan holding the repository (its job
#       queued while the scan ran in the same resource group, as the plan's log
#       names it: none since looper#70; else that queue is a VM's, looper#92),
#       each with what would have let it run in
#       parallel. Per manager (looper#70, looper-<repo>-manager; looper#72):
#       its report-to-merge time (a land queued to merged), the hours lands
#       waited for it, the conflicts it resolved by regenerating against those
#       it sent back, red gates, lands left for the next manager, redone work
#       (a land again after a send-back or red), and from GitHub each merge's
#       build status (a red main after it) and the reverts on main. The
#       concurrency (looper#107: no per-repository cap), from the kicker's
#       capacity lines, in all and per repository: how many reports were active
#       and running at once (peak and mean), the hours ready issues waited with
#       no report job, and the merge backlog. The capacity: the
#       queue p90 of every job, the issues whose report waited for a VM and
#       one more VM's cost a day, the numbers for a manual scale-up. The
#       looper image (LOOPER_RECAP_IMAGE, ghcr.io/tacplus-dev/
#       looper:main) against main's head (looper#58): its revision label, the
#       commits of main not in it and the latest image workflow run; stale for
#       LOOPER_RECAP_IMAGE_HOURS (2) is over. Per runner: the VMs its jobs
#       ran on, the jobs that failed before one was ready, the prepare times
#       (boot to ready: every job has a fresh VM, looper#42) and the disk use the
#       jobs logged at their end. GitHub Actions runs of the workflows named like
#       LOOPER_RECAP_WORKFLOWS (default nightly|release|image) in every
#       repository of LOOPER_ORG. Cloud Build is not read: nothing builds
#       there (looper#85). A source it
#       cannot read is named in `sources`, never guessed. Then its `items`: what
#       went wrong or crossed a threshold, each with a key the nights share.
#   looper-recap classify NIGHT.json [--history DIR]
#       the night's items against the 6 nights before it (DIR/<YYYY-MM-DD>.json,
#       the roadmap's suggestions/recap/): recurring (seen on 2 or more of the 7
#       nights, or over a threshold tonight: advice, ranked by the hours of
#       waiting each would remove; a cap's only once over its threshold its
#       `running` nights running, 2), transient (seen once, under
#       every threshold, and recovered on retry: one line, no advice), or once
#       (seen once, under every threshold, not recovered: one line, no advice;
#       recurring if it comes back). JSON on stdout.
#   looper-recap report NIGHT.json CLASSES.json [--suggestions DIR] [--covered FILE]
#       the night's recap for Jody's daily report, suggestions/recap/<night>.md,
#       on stdout: the one-screen summary, the recurring items with links to the
#       night's advice suggestions in DIR (their `## Advice` names the night and
#       the item) or the issue that covers them (FILE: `<key> <issue> <why>`
#       lines), the transient and once-only items one line each.
#
# Thresholds (environment): LOOPER_RECAP_COUNT (3: failures of a class, in a
# night), LOOPER_RECAP_QUEUE_P90 (900 s), LOOPER_RECAP_PREPARE_P90 (300 s),
# LOOPER_RECAP_HOLD_HOURS (1: an issue held back that long), LOOPER_RECAP_LAND_MINUTES
# (60), LOOPER_RECAP_SCAN_MINUTES (60), LOOPER_RECAP_DISK_PCT (85),
# LOOPER_RECAP_MERGE_MINUTES (120: a manager's report to merge p90),
# LOOPER_RECAP_WAIT_HOURS (1: ready issues waiting with no report job),
# LOOPER_RECAP_BACKLOG (3: lands queued, p90 of the passes: the manager behind),
# LOOPER_RECAP_VM_WAIT (300 s: a report's wait for a VM that counts),
# LOOPER_RECAP_VM_WAIT_HOURS (1). One more VM: LOOPER_RECAP_VM_MACHINE
# (t2a-standard-8) at LOOPER_RECAP_VM_USD_HOUR (0.308, its on-demand list
# price; set it from the billing). LOOPER_RECAP_PASS (180 s, the kicker's
# LOOPER_INTERVAL), LOOPER_RECAP_BUILD_CONTEXT (tacplus-dev/build).
# Environment: GITLAB_TOKEN (read_api; sent on stdin, never on a command line),
# LOOPER_BUILD_API (https://gitlab.com/api/v4), LOOPER_RECAP_PAGES (10 pages of
# 100 jobs a project at most), LOOPER_ORG (tacplus-dev), GH_TOKEN for gh,
# LOOPER_RECAP_ACTIONS_REPOS (default: every repository of LOOPER_ORG).
# LOOPER_RECAP_BUILD_PROJECT (tacplus-dev/build) is read with GITLAB_TOKEN, its
# bot a Reporter there (looper#85; tacplus-dev/cloud#12, option B), or with a
# token of its own read from Secret Manager by version when
# LOOPER_RECAP_BUILD_TOKEN_SECRET (projects/<project>/secrets/<secret>/versions/
# <version>) is set, with gcloud's own login (option A).
